{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the fourteen wells and the communal grain stores in the Velmir Valley violated the prohibition in Additional Protocol I art.54(2) against attacking objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population.",
      "reasoning": "Article 54(2) expressly forbids the attack, destruction, removal or rendering useless of objects such as drinking‑water installations and foodstuffs when the purpose is to deny them to civilians, \"whatever the motive\". The Drennick order to \"fill or poison all wells and burn all grain stores\" was issued expressly to leave nothing for the returning civilian population, satisfying the purpose element of the prohibition. The wells and grain stores were the sole sources of water and winter food for 6,000 civilians, making them objects indispensable to their survival. Accordingly, the conduct falls squarely within the prohibited category.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54(2)",
          "quoted_text": "It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ... for the specific purpose of denying them for their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse Party, whatever the motive"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The exception in Additional Protocol I art.54(3) does not apply because the wells and grain stores were not used for the sustenance of Drennick’s armed forces nor for direct support of military action.",
      "reasoning": "Paragraph 3(a) limits the exception to objects used \"as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces\" and 3(b) to objects used \"in direct support of military action\", with the further condition that such actions must not leave the civilian population with inadequate food or water. The facts state that Drennick troops relied on their own logistics chain and did not use the wells or stores for any military purpose. Destroying them therefore does not meet either sub‑paragraph, and the additional safeguard in 3(b) is breached because the civilian population was left without water and food, causing starvation risk.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "The prohibitions in paragraph 2 shall not apply to such of the objects covered by it as are used by an adverse Party: (a) as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces; or (b) if not as sustenance, then in direct support of military action, provided, however, that in no event shall actions against these objects be taken which may be expected to leave the civilian population with such inadequate food or water as to cause its starvation or force its movement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the wells and grain stores constituted a prohibited reprisal under Additional Protocol I art.54(4).",
      "reasoning": "Paragraph 4 unequivocally states that objects indispensable to civilian survival \"shall not be made the object of reprisals\". The written order explicitly aimed to punish the civilian population by leaving them without water and food as Drennick withdrew, which is the very definition of a reprisal against civilians. Hence the conduct breaches the absolute ban on reprisals.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "These objects shall not be made the object of reprisals."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The derogation provision in Additional Protocol I art.54(5) cannot be invoked by Drennick to justify the destruction.",
      "reasoning": "Paragraph 5 allows derogation \"by a Party to the conflict within such territory under its own control\" for imperative military necessity in defence of its national territory. At the time of the destruction, Drennick was withdrawing from occupied Calvaran territory and no longer exercised effective control over the Velmir Valley. Moreover, the necessity was not linked to defence of Drennick’s own territory but to denying resources to the adversary, which the text does not permit. Consequently, the derogation clause is inapplicable.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "In recognition of the vital requirements of any Party to the conflict in the defence of its national territory against invasion, derogation from the prohibitions contained in paragraph 2 may be made by a Party to the conflict within such territory under its own control where required by imperative military necessity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conduct also breaches the customary rule prohibiting starvation of civilians, as reflected in Additional Protocol II art.14.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international humanitarian law, codified in AP II art.14, forbids starvation as a method of warfare and bars attacks on objects indispensable to civilian survival for that purpose. The deliberate poisoning of wells and burning of grain stores was undertaken to deprive civilians of water and food, satisfying the prohibited purpose. This reinforces the violation established under AP I.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_ii",
          "article": "14",
          "quoted_text": "Starvation of civilians as a method of combat is prohibited. It is therefore prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless, for that purpose, objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ..."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

